etymology_text:
From Proto-Turkic *k(i)aya (“cliff”).
Cognate with Old Uyghur [script needed] (qaya, “rock, cliff”);
Uzbek qoya (“rock, cliff”), Turkmen gaýa (“rock, cliff”), Azerbaijani qaya (“rock”), Khakas хая (xaya, “rock”), Tuvan хая (xaya, “rock”), Yakut хайа (qaya, “mountain”), Kazakh қыя (qıya, “rock, cliff”).

Derived from Proto-Turkic *sebinč (“joy”), from Proto-Turkic *sebin- (“to rejoice”).
Compare cognates in other Turkic languages, which have exactly analogous meanings:
Siberian Tatar сөйөнцө (söyöntsö),
Kazakh сүйінші (süyinši), Kyrgyz сүйүнчү (süyüncü);
Southern Altai сӱгӱнчи (sügünči, “joy, rejoicing”) / сӱӱнчи (süünči);
Uzbek suyunchi / sevinchi, Uyghur سۇيۇنچە (suyunche, “good news; the gift for delivering such news”).
